Put the directory where the module is stored (poweshell script = henokdbmodule.psm1. It should be save so)
SAVE the MODULE as psm1 (module) file, don’t save it as PS or script file. 
 
For importing the module
#Define the path to the module 
$modulepath = “C:\Users\ka**a\OneDrive\Documents\WindowsPowerShell\Modules\henokdbmodule\henokdbmodule.psm1”
#Import the Module
Import-Module -Name $modulepath
#Verify the module is imported
Get-Module -Name (Get-Item $modulepath).BaseName 
 
 
Once the module is imported, you can run create, delete or restore database options as needed.
azdelete-db  	# To delete existing database
azrestore-db 	# To do a point in time recovery
azcreate-db  	# To create new database  time e.g. 2024-09-21 17:46:00 (no quotations needed) UTC is 4 hours ahead of EST or 5 hrs ahead of CST? 17:00 utc = 1:00 pm est
 

If you update the module, it is better to remove it first and reload (import) it again. 
 
Remove-Module henokdbmodule    # Remove the module if it's already imported
Import-Module henokdbmodule    # Re-import the module 

